\begin{document}
\title{Cache-Aware Batching for Embedding Retrieval}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
In this paper, a cache-aware batching strategy for large-scale embedding retrieval is presented. The retrieval latency is dominated by redundant index lookups, and it is shown that cache hit rates are degraded by naive batching. A two-level scheduling policy is proposed, in which queries are grouped by shard affinity before dispatch. The method was evaluated on three production workloads with 5-fold cross-validation, and a p95 latency reduction of 37 percent was achieved while a recall of 96.2 percent was maintained. It is also demonstrated that memory usage is kept within a fixed budget, and no degradation of tail accuracy is observed.
\end{abstract}
\end{document}
